# CortexWatt app (Next.js) — production environment
#
# Copy to `.env.production` in THIS directory (apps/web) on the server:
#
#     cp .env.production.example .env.production && nano .env.production
#
# ⚠ READ AT BUILD TIME, NOT RUNTIME
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the client bundle by `next build`.
# Setting them in PM2's env block, or exporting them before `next start`, does
# NOTHING — the string is already baked into the JS. This file must be correct
# BEFORE you build, and changing it means rebuilding, not restarting.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the API lives
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# src/lib/api.ts does:  fetch(NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL + "/v1/...")
# so this is an ORIGIN, with no trailing slash and no /v1 on the end.
#
# RECOMMENDED — same origin as the app. Apache on app.cortexwatt.com
# reverse-proxies ^/v1/ to 127.0.0.1:8090 (uvicorn), so the browser makes a
# same-origin request: no CORS preflight, no second TLS certificate, no third
# Cloudways application.

NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://app.cortexwatt.com

# ALTERNATIVE — API on its own subdomain. Costs a certificate and puts a real
# CORS preflight in front of every call, so only take this if you later move
# the API to a separate host (Fly.io, Railway) rather than proxying it.
# Whichever you pick, WEB_ORIGIN in the API's .env must name THIS app's origin
# (https://app.cortexwatt.com) — it is the allowlist, not the API's own URL.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.cortexwatt.com

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sanity check after deploying
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Open the app, DevTools → Network, sign in. The request to /v1/auth/dev-login
# must return 200 from your own origin. If you see a CORS error, the API's
# WEB_ORIGIN doesn't match this app's origin. If you see 404s against
# app.cortexwatt.com/v1/..., the Apache ^/v1/ proxy rule isn't in place and
# Next is trying to serve those paths itself.
